    73	suffers from the "state explosion problem" [ 8 ]. Beyond model checking, testing [ 25 ] or fuzzing [ 26 ]
    74	approaches have also been applied to software systems capturing real-world implementations to find
    75	temporal logic property violations. While effectively finding bugs, they rely on higher-quality test
    76	suits or longer execution times to achieve better code coverage. Static analyzers can help mitigate
    77	the above problems, but no existing static analyzer is specifically designed for analyzing temporal
    78	properties. To allow systematic code coverage and effectively finding bugs, we are interested in
    79	developing the first compositional temporal-property-based static analyzer, where each part of the
    80	program is analyzed locally and independently of the global context. It is known that breaking the
    81	large analysis of a whole program into small analyzes of its procedures gives us the ability to scale
    82	independently of the size of the analyzed code [ 35 ]. Here, we highlight three main difficulties in
    83	building a compositional static analyzer to detect temporal property violations:
    84	
    85	(1) To check program behaviors against given properties, existing works [ 7, 25, 26 ] rely on
    86	inclusion checkers from deterministic finite automaton. However, the automata-based approach is
    87	not only complex but also prevents the analysis from being modular [1] due to the lack of high-level
    88	compositional patterns for hierarchical design;
    89	(2) Having all the procedures in question, the formal specifications (abbreviated using specs
    90	from here) of each procedure in question is not always available, and writing specs for them is
    91	unnecessarily tedious and challenging. It is worth mentioning that it is not new for static analyzers
    92	to automatically generate specs. For example, the Facebook’s Infer tool [ 5 ] utilizes bi-abduction
    93	
    94	[ 23 ] to infer pre/post specs from bare code, given the specs for the primitives at the base level of the
    95	code. Hence, the human does not need to write pre/post-conditions for all the procedures, which is
    96	the key to achieving a high level of automation. However, simply developing a bi-abduction for
    97	temporal properties is not sufficient for temporal property analysis;
    98	(3) The classic pre/post-conditions only provide constraints for behaviors before the function
    99	call, and behaviours expected from the current function call, respectively, but we cannot easily
