   178	before freeing the (non-null) input pointer `ptr`, it has to have an event `malloc(ptr)` that occurred
   179	in its past history. We use ( 휋→휃 ) as a short-hand for ( 휋∧휃 ∨¬휋∧_ [★] ). Its postcondition says if `ptr`
   180	is null, then the function does nothing, i.e., 휖 ; and if it is not null, its post-condition captures an
   181	event `free(ptr)` . Its future-condition enforces that after freeing it and within its lifetime, globally
   182	the identifier cannot be used by any events, i.e., G ( `!_(ptr)` ), which is used to prevent null pointer
   183	dereference, double-free and use-after-free. Similarly, the precondition of `malloc` requires its input argument `size` to be positive, and it can be called at any point of the execution, i.e., _ [★] . Its
   184	postcondition states that, when a pointer is successfully allocated, its postcondition captures an
   185	event `malloc(ret)`, where `ret` denotes the return value. Lastly, its future-condition enforces that
   186	the allocated pointer should be finally freed, which is used to prevent memory-leak. Although
   187	simple, these six lines of specs already cover the major memory usage bugs.
